# Tests (Laravel 12 / PHPUnit)

Money-path test coverage lives in `tests/Feature/`:

- `ScanFlowTest` — POST /scan/{game}: happy path, insufficient funds, disabled game, radar offline, atomicity, auth.
- `WalletTransactionInvariantsTest` — model boot hooks: type lowercasing + amount/cost match (1¢ tolerance).
- `AuthFlowTest` — register, login, logout, under-18 rejection, unique phone.
- `Api/ApiEndpointsTest` — /games, /me, /me/game, /winners (auth, pagination, hard cap).
- `MassAssignmentGuardTest` — regression for $fillable lockdown (wallet_balance / role / current_amount can't be mass-assigned).
- `HealthAndLocaleTest` — /db-check auth + locale switching.

Run `composer test` before pushing. When you add money-touching code, add a
test before merging.

If you're new to Laravel testing, the headline is: Laravel ships PHPUnit
configured and ready. Run all tests with `composer test`. Run one file with
`php artisan test --filter=AuthenticationTest`. The first time you run them,
the test runner uses an **in-memory SQLite database** (config'd in
`phpunit.xml`), so your real `database/database.sqlite` is untouched.

## Two flavors of test

```
tests/
├── Feature/    HTTP-level tests. Hit a route, assert response. Use the DB.
├── Unit/       Pure logic tests. No DB, no HTTP, no Laravel. Just PHP.
└── TestCase.php    Base class — extend this in feature tests
```

If you're testing "what happens when a user POSTs to /scan/3", that's a
**Feature test**. If you're testing "does this currency formatter return the
right string for negative values", that's a **Unit test**.

When in doubt, write a Feature test. Real bugs live in the integration
between layers — controller, model, DB — and Feature tests cover all three.

## Anatomy of a good Feature test

```php
namespace Tests\Feature;

use App\Models\Game;
use App\Models\User;
use App\Models\WalletTransaction;
use Illuminate\Foundation\Testing\RefreshDatabase;
use Tests\TestCase;

class ScanTest extends TestCase
{
    use RefreshDatabase;  // Wipes DB between tests

    /** @test */
    public function a_user_can_scan_a_game_they_can_afford(): void
    {
        // Arrange
        $game = Game::factory()->create(['price_to_play' => 4, 'name' => 'Bike & Electronics']);
        $user = User::factory()->create(['wallet_balance' => 10]);

        // Act
        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->post("/scan/{$game->id}");

        // Assert
        $response->assertOk();

        $this->assertDatabaseHas('scans', [
            'user_id' => $user->id,
            'game_id' => $game->id,
            'cost'    => 4,
        ]);

        $this->assertDatabaseHas('wallet_transactions', [
            'user_id' => $user->id,
            'game_id' => $game->id,
            'amount'  => 4,
            'type'    => 'debit',
        ]);

        $this->assertEquals(6, $user->fresh()->wallet_balance);
    }

    /** @test */
    public function a_user_cannot_scan_if_they_cannot_afford_it(): void
    {
        $game = Game::factory()->create(['price_to_play' => 32]);
        $user = User::factory()->create(['wallet_balance' => 5]);

        $response = $this->actingAs($user)->post("/scan/{$game->id}");

        $response->assertStatus(422);
        $this->assertDatabaseCount('scans', 0);
        $this->assertDatabaseCount('wallet_transactions', 0);
    }
}
```

The pattern: **Arrange → Act → Assert.** Three blocks, each one or two lines
unless it's a complex setup.

## Factories you have available

Models with factories (in `database/factories/`):
- `UserFactory` — public users (web guard)
- `GameFactory` — the five games
- `ScanFactory`, `WinnerFactory`, `WalletTransactionFactory`, `DrawFactory`

Use them; don't `Model::create([...])` in tests. Factories are how you
declare "I need a fixture, give me one" without spelling out every required
field.

```php
// Good
$user = User::factory()->create(['wallet_balance' => 100]);

// Bad — verbose, breaks when migrations add required columns
$user = User::create([
    'name' => 'Test', 'phone' => '...', 'password' => '...',
    'wallet_balance' => 100, /* every other required field */
]);
```

## Asserting against the DB

| Helper | Use when |
|---|---|
| `assertDatabaseHas('table', [...])` | "A row with these values exists" |
| `assertDatabaseMissing('table', [...])` | "No row with these values" |
| `assertDatabaseCount('table', N)` | "Exactly N rows in this table" |
| `assertModelExists($model)` | "This particular Eloquent instance still exists" |
| `assertModelMissing($model)` | "This was deleted" |

For wallet/scan tests, use `assertDatabaseHas('wallet_transactions', ...)`
rather than fetching with `WalletTransaction::where(...)` — the helper has
better diff output when it fails.

## Authentication in tests

```php
$user = User::factory()->create();

// Web guard (public users)
$this->actingAs($user)->get('/dashboard');

// Admin guard (Filament)
$admin = AdminUser::factory()->create();
$this->actingAs($admin, 'admin')->get('/admin');
```

For routes that need auth, `actingAs($user)` swaps in a logged-in user for
the duration of the test. No need to POST to /login.

## Don't run real migrations against your dev DB

`phpunit.xml` is configured to use **`:memory:` SQLite** for tests. That's
why `RefreshDatabase` is fast and doesn't delete your local seed data. If
you ever see tests touching `database/database.sqlite`, something is
misconfigured — open `phpunit.xml` and check `DB_CONNECTION=sqlite` and
`DB_DATABASE=:memory:` are set in the `<php>` block.

## What to test first (priority order)

1. **`Game::attemptScan()`** — the core money-moving method. Test:
   - happy path (user can afford → scan happens, wallet drops, transaction created)
   - insufficient funds → 422, no scan, no transaction
   - duplicate scan_id → second one returns the existing transaction (`firstOrCreate`)
   - failed scan still creates a transaction (`scan.success = false` but row exists)

2. **Auth flows** — `RegisteredUserController`, `AuthenticatedSessionController`.
   Test:
   - register with valid phone + password → user created, logged in
   - login with wrong password → 422
   - logout clears the session

3. **Admin top-up action** — the `topoffRadarCash` action in `UserResource`.
   Test:
   - admin tops up → wallet rises, transaction has `game_id = null`, `type = topup`
   - non-admin can't reach it

4. **Wallet invariants** (the `WalletTransaction::boot()` rules):
   - saving with mismatched amount/cost throws / fails
   - saving with `'TOPUP'` normalizes to `'topup'`

## Don't

- **Don't share state between tests.** That's what `RefreshDatabase` is for.
  If you find yourself writing `setUp()` that depends on the previous test,
  rethink.
- **Don't seed in tests.** Factories make exactly the data you need.
- **Don't sleep.** No `sleep(1)`, no `usleep()`. If you're waiting on async
  work, use `Bus::fake()` / `Queue::fake()` / `Mail::fake()`.
- **Don't make HTTP calls to external services.** Mock with `Http::fake()`.
- **Don't catch and `assertTrue(true)`.** Either expect a specific exception
  with `$this->expectException(...)` or assert the actual outcome.
